{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785868889,"nonce":"GDUBKG+jupWQ6+46JxcpI0o+kME=","title":"b4 prep --enforce-deps: materialize declared series dependencies locally","message":"Reported-by: Maurice Hieronymus \u003cmhi@mailbox.org\u003e\nLink: https://lore.kernel.org/20260711081938.59972-1-mhi@mailbox.org\n\n\nb4 prep can declare series dependencies (--edit-deps) and verify them\n(--check-deps), but never materializes them: --check-deps applies\neverything in a throwaway check and discards the result. Users working\non stacked series still stack branches by hand, and re-stack by hand\nevery time a prerequisite gets a new revision.\n\nProposed:\n\n    b4 prep --enforce-deps\n\nreads the declared prerequisites, figures out what needs to be rebased\non what, and runs the git commands to bring the local branch into the\nstate the dependencies describe (base-commit -\u003e prereq series -\u003e own\npatches). Conflicts stop the rebase as usual and are the user's\nproblem. Since an enforce can involve several steps:\n\n    b4 prep --abort-enforce-deps\n\nrestores the pre-enforce state after a partially completed enforce,\nwhich means saving the branch head and tracking state to a backup ref\nbefore touching anything.\n\nReporter offered to prototype and send patches.","files":null}]}